(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-3-pelvic-exam-quiz"]={"title":"Pelvic Exam (Female Genitalia) — Set 1 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","questions":[{"q":"What is the mons pubis?","choices":["Hair-covered fat pads overlying the symphysis pubis, with an inverted-triangle hair distribution","The area located between the vaginal introitus and the anus, bounded by the thighs","The thin, pink-red labial folds that extend anteriorly to form the clitoral prepuce","The thin membrane that partially covers the introitus in a virginal female"],"answer":0,"exp":"The mons pubis is a hair-covered fat pad over the symphysis pubis with an inverted-triangle hair pattern and a flat base extending onto the abdomen. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"What are the labia minora?","choices":["Rounded folds of adipose and connective tissue extending from the mons to past the posterior commissure","Hair-covered fat pads overlying the symphysis pubis","Thinner, pink-red folds that extend anteriorly to form the prepuce","A fossa that lies medial to the labia minora"],"answer":2,"exp":"The labia minora are thin, pink-red folds that extend anteriorly to form the clitoral prepuce. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the vestibule?","choices":["A fossa that lies medial to the labia minora","The cutaneous covering of the clitoris","The area between the introitus and the anus","The membrane covering the introitus in a virginal female"],"answer":0,"exp":"The vestibule is the fossa medial to the labia minora, containing the urethral meatus and vaginal introitus. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"Where are Bartholin's glands located, and what are they also known as?","choices":["Pockets formed by the folded vaginal wall as it surrounds the cervix opening","Periurethral glands with openings on either side of the urethral meatus","Located at the 5 and 7 o'clock positions, also known as the greater vestibular glands","The domed superior area of the uterine body, above the fallopian tube insertions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Bartholin's glands (greater vestibular glands) have openings between the labia minora and vaginal orifice, located at approximately the 5 and 7 o'clock positions. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"What are Skene's glands?","choices":["Periurethral glands with openings on either side of the urethral meatus","The membrane covering the introitus in a virginal female","The erectile tissue and glans found at the tip of the clitoris","The greater vestibular glands located at 5 and 7 o'clock"],"answer":0,"exp":"Skene's glands are periurethral glands whose openings lie on either side of the urethral meatus. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"Where does the vagina lie anatomically?","choices":["Lateral to the labia majora","Superior to the uterine fundus","Between the ovaries and fallopian tubes","Between the bladder and the rectum"],"answer":3,"exp":"The vagina is a hollow muscular tube lying between the bladder and the rectum. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the vaginal fornix?","choices":["The thin membrane that partially covers the vaginal introitus","The domed superior portion of the uterine body, above the tubal insertions","The intravaginal portion of the cervix that protrudes into the vagina","Pockets made by the folded vaginal wall surrounding the cervix"],"answer":3,"exp":"The fornices are pockets formed by the folded vaginal wall around the cervix. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the fundus of the uterus?","choices":["The distal, fan-like end of the fallopian tube","The intravaginal, visible portion of the cervix","The domed superior area of the uterine body","The retrouterine peritoneal pouch"],"answer":2,"exp":"The fundus is the domed superior area of the uterine body. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is the Pouch of Douglas, and how is it clinically accessible?","choices":["The external cervical os, a small opening reachable only on direct visual inspection with a speculum","A pocket of the vaginal wall that surrounds the cervix, reachable directly on speculum examination","The fan-like fimbriated end of the fallopian tube, reachable only on bimanual palpation deep in the adnexa","The retrouterine pouch/cul-de-sac formed by peritoneum extending from the uterus onto the rectum, reachable on rectal exam"],"answer":3,"exp":"The Pouch of Douglas (retrouterine pouch) is a peritoneal cul-de-sac between the uterus and rectum, accessible on rectal examination. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 17"},{"q":"At what level are the ovaries suspended?","choices":["At the level of the introitus","At the level of the symphysis pubis","At the level of the umbilicus","ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The small, almond-shaped ovaries are suspended from the oviducts and uterus at the level of the ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine). — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 16"},{"q":"Why should the patient empty her bladder before the pelvic exam?","choices":["It prevents contamination of the pelvic exam equipment and sterile instrument tray","A full bladder compromises the bimanual exam, impedes palpation of organs, and creates discomfort","It has no real clinical effect on exam quality, and emptying the bladder is only a courtesy","It is only necessary if a Pap smear and endocervical sample will be performed"],"answer":1,"exp":"An empty bladder is important because a full bladder compromises the bimanual exam, impedes organ palpation, and causes patient discomfort. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"Why should the drape be depressed at the midline during the pelvic exam?","choices":["It is not a recommended technique in modern examination practice","To improve the overhead lighting angle for the exam","To allow eye contact between the patient and examiner","To keep the sterile instrument tray free of contamination"],"answer":2,"exp":"Depressing the drape's midline allows the patient and examiner to maintain eye contact, aiding relaxation and communication. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 19"},{"q":"Why should the patient's arms never be positioned over her head during the pelvic exam?","choices":["It increases the risk of vasovagal syncope","This position tenses the abdominal muscles","It is uncomfortable but has no effect on the exam","It obstructs the examiner's view of the perineum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Arms overhead tense the abdominal muscles, which is counterproductive to the relaxation needed for the exam. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"Why is contact with the inner thighs made before touching the genitalia?","choices":["It prepares the woman for the exam before genital contact occurs","It helps the examiner position the stirrups at the correct height","It has no specific purpose at all, it is purely incidental contact","It is required to assess bilateral femoral pulses before proceeding"],"answer":0,"exp":"Making contact with the inner thighs first prepares the patient for the upcoming genital exam, reducing surprise/tension. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"Why should a water-soluble lubricant, rather than a gel lubricant, be used for the speculum?","choices":["A gel lubricant will interfere with the Pap smear","Gel lubricants cause more patient discomfort","There is no difference; either lubricant type is acceptable","Gel lubricants are more expensive"],"answer":0,"exp":"Gel lubricants can interfere with Pap smear cytology results, so water-soluble lubricant is specifically used instead. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"What should be assessed regarding maturation during the external pelvic exam?","choices":["Only the patient's menstrual history","Tanner staging (sexual maturity rating)","Bone age assessed via wrist imaging","Only chronological age from the chart"],"answer":1,"exp":"Assessing maturation via Tanner staging is part of the external pelvic exam. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"According to Tanner staging of pubarche, what characterizes Stage 3?","choices":["Sparse growth of long, slightly pigmented hair at the base of the labia","Prepubertal, with only vellus hair similar to the abdominal wall","Adult in type and quantity, spread with a horizontal upper border","Darker, coarser, more curled hair spreading sparsely over the junction of the pubes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Stage 3 pubarche is characterized by darker, coarser, curlier hair spreading sparsely over the pubic junction. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"How is the technique for separating the labia to expose internal structures performed?","choices":["Using two cotton swabs held gently against the labial folds","Using the index finger and thumb to spread the labia minora laterally","Using a speculum blade to spread the tissue before manual inspection","This step is unnecessary whenever a speculum will later be used"],"answer":1,"exp":"The labia minora are spread laterally using the index finger and thumb to expose the clitoris, urethral meatus, and introitus. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"How are the Bartholin's glands palpated?","choices":["Insert two fingers fully into the vagina and sweep them laterally across the wall from the introitus to the fornices, checking for masses","Insert the index finger into the vagina at the posterior introitus, keeping the thumb external, and palpate between thumb and index finger at the 5 and 7 o'clock positions","Palpate externally only, pressing along the labia majora and perineal skin, without ever inserting a finger into the vagina at all","Use the open speculum to visualize the gland openings directly and press externally on them without any digital palpation of the tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"This bidigital technique (internal index finger, external thumb) at the 5 and 7 o'clock positions is used to palpate the Bartholin's glands. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 26"},{"q":"How are Skene's glands assessed if needed?","choices":["They must be \"milked\" from the interior","They are visualized directly with the speculum","They are assessed only via ultrasound","They cannot be assessed on physical exam"],"answer":0,"exp":"Skene's glands are assessed by milking them from the interior if needed, given their periurethral location. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 26"},{"q":"Before speculum insertion, what preliminary step helps identify the cervix's position?","choices":["Performing a full rectal exam first to estimate the uterine position and size before insertion","Auscultating the lower abdomen with a stethoscope to listen for bowel sounds and vascular bruits beforehand","Insertion of the index finger to identify the cervix by feel (firm, round surface), aiding the angle of speculum insertion","Percussing the lower abdomen and suprapubic region to estimate bladder fullness and organomegaly first"],"answer":2,"exp":"A preliminary index finger insertion helps locate/guesstimate the cervix position, which aids proper speculum angle. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 28"},{"q":"At what angle and along which wall is the closed speculum introduced?","choices":["No specific angle matters at all, any insertion approach works equally well","Downward angle of 45 degrees, along the posterior wall, then vertical","Upward angle of 45 degrees, along the anterior vaginal wall","Straight horizontal insertion from the very first moment of contact"],"answer":1,"exp":"The closed speculum is introduced at a 45-degree downward angle along the posterior vaginal wall, oriented vertically. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 30"},{"q":"When is the speculum rotated from vertical to horizontal orientation?","choices":["Before insertion begins","Immediately upon initial contact with the introitus","Once the full length of the speculum is inserted","Rotation is never necessary"],"answer":2,"exp":"The speculum is rotated to horizontal only after it has been fully inserted vertically along the posterior wall. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 30"},{"q":"What technique brings the cervix into full view once the speculum is opened?","choices":["Asking the patient to cough","Applying suprapubic pressure","Withdrawing the speculum completely and reinserting","\"Cupping\" of the cervix"],"answer":3,"exp":"Opening the speculum and \"cupping\" the cervix brings it into full view for inspection. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is the key difference between the conventional Pap smear and the ThinPrep Pap Test?","choices":["ThinPrep does not require a speculum exam at all, only a blind vaginal swab for collection of the sample","The way cells are placed on the slide, with ThinPrep minimizing cell overlap, blood, mucus, and inflammation obscuring cells","ThinPrep eliminates the need for a cervical brush or spatula, relying only on a single cotton swab for collection","ThinPrep is a less expensive alternative that uses cheaper slide-preparation chemicals than the smear"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cells are collected and reviewed the same way in both methods; ThinPrep differs in how cells are placed on the slide, reducing obscuring artifacts. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 36"},{"q":"During the bimanual exam, what is assessed when palpating the cervix and fornices for movement?","choices":["Any movement at all is abnormal","Movement is not assessed during the bimanual exam","Normal movement is 1-2cm without pain","Only pain is assessed, not the degree of movement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Cervical/fornix movement of 1-2cm without pain is the normal finding on bimanual exam. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 39"},{"q":"How is the uterus palpated bimanually?","choices":["Only the pelvic hand is used inside the vagina, with no abdominal hand pressing down externally at all","The uterus cannot reliably be palpated on bimanual exam in any patient regardless of body habitus","Only the abdominal hand presses down externally, with no vaginal finger insertion performed at any point","Free hand on the abdomen presses down while the pelvic hand elevates the cervix, \"grasping\" the uterus between the two hands"],"answer":3,"exp":"This bimanual technique — abdominal hand pressing down, pelvic hand elevating the cervix — allows the uterus to be grasped between the two hands. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 40"},{"q":"How are the ovaries assessed during the bimanual exam?","choices":["Only the pelvic hand is used, pressing directly and firmly against the cervix, with no abdominal hand ever pressing to trap the ovary","Abdominal hand on the lower quadrant while the pelvic hand is in the corresponding fornix, pressing lightly and pushing adnexal structures between the hands","Both hands remain flat on the abdomen only, palpating the lower quadrants from outside without any vaginal finger or fornix insertion","Ovaries are always easily palpable in every patient regardless of body habitus, obesity, prior surgery, or bowel gas present"],"answer":1,"exp":"This is the correct technique — abdominal hand on the lower quadrant, pelvic hand in the corresponding fornix, to palpate adnexal structures. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 40"},{"q":"How is pelvic muscular strength tested at the end of the bimanual exam?","choices":["This assessment is not part of the standard bimanual exam, and is deferred entirely to a specialized pelvic floor therapy referral scheduled as a separate visit","Measure vaginal wall thickness with a set of small calipers, comparing readings at three points along the canal, then repeating after a five-minute rest","Ask the patient to cough forcefully three times while the examining fingers remain at the cervix, checking for descent of the pelvic organs or urinary leakage","Withdraw fingers to clear the cervix, spread to touch the vaginal wall, and ask the patient to squeeze around the examining fingers, holding for a minimum of 3 seconds at full strength"],"answer":3,"exp":"This describes the correct technique for testing pelvic muscular strength during the bimanual exam. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is the first step of the rectovaginal exam, regarding gloves?","choices":["Use the same glove from the bimanual exam without changing it","Remove the outer glove, since there should be a second glove underneath","Apply a fresh third glove layer directly over the existing two gloves","No glove change is needed between bimanual and rectovaginal exam"],"answer":1,"exp":"The rectovaginal exam begins by removing the outer (now-contaminated vaginal) glove, revealing a clean second glove underneath. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 44"}]};
